ABSTRACT. Memristors (MRs) are considered promising devices with enormous potential to replace Complementary Metal-Oxide-Semiconductor (CMOS) technology, which approaches the scale limit. Efforts to fabricate MRs-based hybrid materials may result in suitable operating parameters coupled to high mechanical flexibility and low-cost. Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs) arise as a favorable candidate to cover such demands. The step-by-step growth of MOFs structures on functionalized surfaces, called Surface-Supported Metal-Organic Frameworks (SURMOFs), opens the possibility for designing new applications in strategic fields such as electronics, optoelectronics, and energy harvesting. However, considering the MRs architecture, the typical high porosity of these hybrid materials may lead to short-circuited devices easily. In this sense, here is reported for the first time the integration of SURMOF films in rolled-up scalablefunctional devices. A freestanding metallic nanomembrane provides a robust and self-adjusted top mechanical contact on the SURMOF layer. The electrical characterization reveals an ambipolar resistive switching mediated by the humidity level with low-power consumption. The electronic properties are investigated with density functional theory (DFT) calculations. Furthermore, the device concept is versatile, compatible with the current parallelism demands of integration, and transcends the challenge in contacting SURMOF films for scalable-functional devices.
Memristors (MRs) are considered promising candidates to replace Complementary MetalOxide-Semiconductor (CMOS) technology, which approaches the scale limit. In such a device, the resistance depends on the charge flowing, and an internal conduction state may be reached with a small amount of energy at the speedy response $\left(<1\right.$ ns).1 The information storage through non-volatile or volatile memory is the main envisioned application.2 The non-volatile memristor retains an internal conduction state even after removal of the externally applied bias voltage,3 making them suitable to data storage systems.4,5 Furthermore, the simple architecture enables the fabrication of small devices ${\it\Omega}^{\prime}{\sim}2{\mathrm{nm}})$ ,6 allowing parallel fabrication and high integration density.
Efforts to fabricate MRs-based hybrid materials are still crucial to correlate operating parameters with high mechanical flexibility and low-cost.7 In such a context, Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs) arise as a favorable candidate to bridge the gap between inorganic and organic systems. Considered as a class of hybrid porous and crystalline materials, MOFs are based on metal ions or clusters connected by organic linkers. They have successfully been used for applications involving catalysis, chemical sensors, gas storage, and separations.8–11 The increased interest in such structures over the past two decades is associated with the possibility of adjusting their porous size and crystallinity through the choice of the organic linkers.12 Additional tailorability, necessary to develop specific applications, may be achieved incorporating to the MOFs pores guest molecules.13–15 Strategies based on post-synthetic modifications can also be employed.16 Furthermore, as a hybrid material, MOFs have high mechanical flexibility.17 Despite its impressive versatility, one of the ongoing challenges to widespread its application in strategic fields such as electronics, optoelectronics, and energy harvesting, is the control the growth of the layer on desired surfaces. The step-by-step growth of these structures on functionalized surfaces, called Surface-Supported Metal-Organic Framework (SURMOFs), has been introduced to obtain highly oriented MOFs layers on modified Ausubstrates.18 Such an approach paved the way for the integration of MOFs as functional films in devices,19 opening possibilities to study and explore their physical and chemical properties.
The resistive switching behavior in MOFs has previously been investigated by Pan et al. in Hong Kong University of Science and Technology (HKUST)-1 structures.20 They used a SURMOF approach to grow a 130-nm-thick layer on Au-coated PET substrates, resulting in Au/HKUST-1/Au PET memory devices. The observed resistive switching was a consequence of the formation of conducting filaments from pyrolyzed organic linkers in addition to the electric field-induced migration of the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions. Despite the irreversible structural effect induced, and low on/off ratio, such devices showed reasonable stability. Wang et al. explored the resistive switching characteristics of HKUST-1 by incorporating ferrocene guest molecules inside the pores,14 resulting in a substantial increase in conductivity through the formation of the Fe ionpercolation pathway (although its low reproducibility and weak resistive switching). Redox reactions from anodic dissolution processes for HKUST-1 were also explored,21 exhibiting good rewritability but a low on/off ratio. Other selected MOFs have been considered for MRs devices, including the Zeolitic Imidazolate Framework (ZIF)-8,22–24 Materials of Institute Lavoisier (MIL)-53,25 Resistance-Switchable Metal-Organic Framework (RSMOF)-1,26 and RubidiumCyclodextrin (Rb-CD),27 zinc ions with tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin (Zn-TCPP) blended with polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVPy),28 and chiral metal-organic framework (FJU-23-H2O).29 However, the combination of reasonable operating parameters, e.g., on/off ratio, retention, and low-power consumption, with the manufacturability, including parallelism and process scalability, are still in its infancy.30
The possibility of controlling SURMOF film characteristics by self-assembling processes results in highly porous frameworks with controlled film thickness, homogeneity, and morphology. These features are particularly interesting for electronic applications. However, the challenges to fabricate reliable functional devices based on such structures are still present.19,31 It is well known that performing reliable electrical contacts to molecular layers is one of the main issues in molecular/organic electronics.32 The complexity becomes visible once thin films are aimed.33 Since most of the reports on SURMOF-based devices employ films with thicknesses of several tens of nanometers,20,22–25 such an issue has not been entirely lifted. Alternatives, e.g., mercury-based tunneling heterojunctions,34 can be used to contact SURMOFs films, but do not allow low-temperature investigation (what is essential to understand the charge transport mechanisms) or effective integration. In this sense, the rolled-up technology may be an exciting alternative to prepare highly integrated and robust devices.
The rolled-up technology has been employed in a variety of functional systems, such as sensors, capacitors, and Organic Field-Effect Transistors (OFETs), with the target in reducing the footprint area.35–37 This technology also has an additional advantage in contacting ultrathin films without damage,38 making possible the contact of films with thicknesses of some nanometers.
Here we report for the first time the fabrication of SURMOF heterojunction using strained Rolled-up Metallic Nanomembranes (rMNs) as the top contact. Such a device structure was fabricated following the approach used in rNMs.38 Besides, this architecture is versatile, compatible with the current parallelism demands of integration. During the fabrication process, the top contact is formed after the self-releasing of the strained metallic nanomembrane through the selective removal of a sacrificial layer. The electrical characterization of the HKUST-1
SURMOF heterojunction showed that by controlling the relative humidity (RH) at room temperature, an ambipolar and high-resistance switching can be modulated with low-power consumption. In order to better understand the underlying physical mechanism, electronic properties were investigated with Density Functional Theory (DFT) calculations, showing the impact of the presence of water with their electronic energy levels that might lead to enhanced electronic mobility. This observation demonstrates that by the effective integration of SURMOF films, novel scalable-functional devices can be created. Furthermore, this device concept transcends the challenge of contacting SURMOF films, extending the possibilities to explore their electrical properties at the nanoscale range.
Figure 1a-b shows an illustration of the device concept before and after the rolled-up process. As mentioned, the rolling-up process of the strained metallic nanomembrane towards SURMOF film is caused by the selective removal of a germanium oxide $(\mathrm{GeO_{x}})$ sacrificial layer in aqueous solution. A detailed description of device fabrication is presented in the Experimental Section and Supporting Information (Figures from S1 to S3). The step-by-step growth of HKUST-1 SURMOF is presented in Figure S4. Based on the fabrication process, using conventional photolithography, this device concept is versatile and can be used to integrate functional structures present on microfabricated wafers. Figure 1c shows the 3D Laser Scanning Confocal Microscopy (LSCM) image of an array of devices fabricated on $\mathrm{Si}/\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ substrate with $9\mathrm{-mm}^{2}.$ - area, evidencing the scalability of 32 devices on a chip. The yield after the rolled-up process is superior to $93\%$ . In Figure 1d, a Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) image of a single Au/SAM-SURMOF/Au rolled-up vertical heterojunction is presented in detail. After the selfreleased of the metallic nanomembrane, a tube-like electrode establishes a top mechanical contact on the SURMOF film, as seen in Figure 1e.
image showing the scalability of the fabrication process. As can be seen, 32 devices on a chip can be fabricated. (d) SEM image of a single Au/SAM-SURMOF/Au rolled-up vertical heterojunction in detail. (e) Sketch of the cross-section of the Au/SAM-SURMOF/Au rolled-up vertical heterojunction.
Figure 2a shows the illustration of a single Au/SAM-SURMOF/Au rolled-up vertical heterojunction; the dotted region refers to the colored SEM image presented in Figure 2b. The tube electrode contacts the SURMOF film from the top, providing a self-adjusted top mechanical contact. The length of the tube electrode is $280\pm10~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , and its diameter $8.0\pm0.4~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (see
Figure S5a-b). Such tube dimensions can be changed by modifying the strain configuration of both Ti and Cr layers,39 as well as the dimensions of the strained metallic layer shown in Figure S2c. These parameters have an influence on the outer diameter of the tube electrode, which may modify the injection-area and the electrical connection to the SURMOF film. However, compared to other strategies,34 this approach is robust and allows the investigation of the devices under different experimental conditions, including at low-temperatures $(<20\mathrm{k})$ .40
Figure 2c shows the Grazing Incidence X-ray Diffraction (GIXRD) patterns of the HKUST-1 SURMOF film grown on the functionalized finger-like electrode. As can be seen, the photolithography process does not affect the SURMOF film. The pattern obtained is consistent with previous results for HKUST-1.18 Peaks found at $6.91^{\mathrm{{o}}}$ and $11.77^{\circ}$ (2θ degree) are the preferential growth orientations of the SURMOF film, corresponding to [100] and [111] directions, respectively.41 The step-by-step growth of SURMOF films takes place from the nucleation phenomena and the formation of oriented crystallites, following the Volmer-Weber mechanism.41,42 The observed growth directions are typical for the Liquid-Phase Epitaxy (LPE) process, and its origin can be related to parameters such as the temperature of deposition, the orientation of the substrate, and uniformity of the MHA (6-Mercaptohexanoic Acid)-SAM (SelfAssembled Monolayer) on the substrate surface. The MHA-SAM is responsible for introducing a –COOH functionality. Also, the [100] and [111] oriented crystallites may result in distinct types of islands.41
The Atomic Force Microscopy (AFM) topography image of a HKUST-1 SURMOF film grown on the functionalized finger electrode is presented in Figure 2d. For an area of $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ x 2 $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , the SURMOF film has a homogeneous surface without significant defects, such as pinholes or agglomerates. The surface roughness, represented by the root mean square (Rq), was found
$3.42\pm0.38\mathrm{nm}$ . The washing steps between the solutions, Figure S4, and the reproducibility of changing solutions during the cycles are critical parameters to obtain continuous and homogeneous SURMOF films. The corresponding thickness of the HKUST-1 SURMOF film is around $20\mathrm{nm}$ , as can be seen in Figure 2e-f. Furthermore, the tube electrode showed a Rq of $3.65\pm0.15\mathrm{{nm}}$ , see Figure S5d. This value is very similar to previous works,38,40 and it must be considered to precisely estimate both geometric and effective device contacting areas (AGEO and AEFF).
In rNMs-based devices, only a small fraction of the geometrically defined region is mechanically contacted. This small fraction is defined as $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{EFF}}$ . However, AGEO and $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{EFF}}$ can differ substantially because of the topography of the involved interfaces.32,40,43,44 AGEO can be estimated following the Hertzian theory for contact mechanics.45 This theory describes the local deformations between two bodies with different geometries. The approach considers that two different elastic half-spaces are placed in close contact, i.e., a hollow cylinder (tube electrode) touching a horizontal plane (finger electrode). Considering the geometric parameters obtained through SEM images in Figure S5b-c, AGEO was found $\sim8~\upmu\mathrm{m}^{2}$ . Taking into account the ratio between the surface roughnesses (regions from 26 to $30\mathrm{nm}$ of height in the $z$ -axis of Figure S6), and the total surface area contacted at the finger electrode, $^{40}\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{EFF}}$ can be estimated as being ${\sim}10^{3}$ $\mathrm{nm}^{2}$ . Both $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{GEO}}$ and $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{EFF}}$ values are in good agreement with previous reports.33,40
Colored SEM image showing the tube electrode in contact with SURMOF after the rolled-up process. (c) GIXRD pattern of the HKUST-1 SURMOF before and after patterning (photolithography process). (d) $2\upmu\mathrm{m}\up x2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ AFM topography image of the SURMOF grown on the finger electrode. (e) Profile SURMOF thickness obtained from the $5\upmu\mathrm{m}\mathrm{n}10\upmu\mathrm{m}$ AFM topography image shown in (f).
Typical I-V characteristics of the Au/SAM-SURMOF/Au rolled-up vertical heterojunction at different RH levels are shown in Figure 3. All curves were obtained by monitoring the current as a function of DC voltage sweeping, at room temperature, and controlled RH. Both, the circuit diagram used for the electrical characterization and the geometric specifications of the contactpads of the rNMs-based devices are presented in Figure S7. Furthermore, the rolled-up architecture is validated in Figure S8, where the current-voltage (I-V) characteristics for parallel plate capacitors-based HKUST-1 SURMOF are presented. As can be seen, for 20 HKUST-1 deposition cycles, an ohmic behavior is observed, indicating that metal evaporation has possibly affected the film because of the thin thickness.
As evidenced in Figure 3a, initially, the device is found at a High-Resistance-State (HRS or OFF state). In such a state, the current exhibits a small increase as the voltage is gradually applied. At a specific threshold voltage $(\mathrm{V_{T}})$ , the current increases abruptly (SET) reaching a Low-Resistance-State (LRS or ON state). The LRS state remains even if the applied voltage is further increased. The current compliance $(10^{-6}\mathrm{A})$ was kept limited to prevent the breakdown of the device during the setting process. Under a reverse applied voltage sweep, the current shows a small decrease followed by an abrupt return to HRS. The same behavior was observed for the opposite polarity. Therefore, the resistive switching is ambipolar, i.e., HRS and LRS can be obtained for both voltage bias within the same cycle. Such ambipolarity is extremely interesting in applications involving memories and neuromorphic systems since it can be used to perform operations as write, read, and erase on both voltage polarities.
Figure 3b shows repeated switching cycles at $90\%$ RH. The resistive switching behavior has good reproducibility over 20 successive cycles, with $\mathrm{v_{T}}$ between 0.7 to $1\mathrm{V}$ . The average on/off ratio is ${\sim}3.6\mathrm{x}10^{5}$ at absolute readout voltage $(\mathrm{V_{READ}})$ of $0.2\mathrm{V}$ for both polarities. By decreasing
RH to $80\%$ , Figure 3c, similar resistive switching behavior was observed, but with a substantial increase of the average on/off ratio to ${\sim}1.7\mathrm{x}10^{7}$ at the very same VREAD. The reason for this increase is associated with the current at HRS state, which is higher for $90\%$ RH when compared to the values found at $80\%$ RH. By further decreasing RH, Figure 3d-f, the switching behavior changes substantially, indicating the existence of an optimum RH value to achieve a maximum on/off ratio. Apart from the switching behavior, $\mathrm{v_{T}}$ shifts towards higher voltages as the number of cycles evolves, while the on/off ratio decreases. The repeatability of the switching behavior, however, remains with the necessary reproducibility for practical purposes. Bellow 40 $\%$ RH, Figure 3e-f, no reproducible resistive switching behavior was achieved.
HKUST-1 is composed of dimeric cupric tetracarboxylic units connected through aromatic rings of 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid (BTC) ligands, where the first is hydrophilic and second hydrophobic.46 Thus, under high RH levels, the electronic characteristics may change significantly. Very recently, its capability of water absorption was employed to improve organic charge carrier density and sensibility in OFETs.47 However, the resistive switching of such material at different humidity levels remains unexplored, evidencing the need for a deeper understanding of the switching mechanism under these conditions.
In order to explore the impact of the water molecules inside the pores of HKUST-1, and its effect on the resistive switching characteristics, DFT calculations were performed. However, differently from what was previously reported,48 here the effect of a single oxygen vacancy, making an analogy to a possible structural defect, was considered (since ideal conditions are impractical). In the first scenario, HKUST-1 was considered pristine, while in a second situation a single oxygen vacancy (Vo) per unit cell around the metal cluster was introduced. In the third and fourth scenarios, one and two water molecules were placed close to the defective metal cluster $\mathrm{(Vo+H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and $\mathrm{Vo}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\$ ). Then, the ionic positions were allowed to relax to an energy minimum for all situations. Afterward, the atomic-species-projected density of states (pDOS) and energy levels real-space projections were obtained, as presented in Figure 4.
The calculated bandgap for pristine HKUST-1 is $1.83\mathrm{eV}$ , Figure 4a, which is compatible with previous theoretical results but underestimated concerning the experimental value of about 3.6 eV.49 This result is expected from DFT calculations at this level of approximation for the exchange-correlation functional.50 Figure 4b shows the pristine HKUST-1 unit cell. By introducing an oxygen vacancy in the metal cluster, the bandgap decreases to about $1\mathrm{eV}$ , and a single electronic state is observed in the bandgap region (the forbidden region), as shown in Figure 4c. This state is attributed to the structural defect intentionally introduced. According to its real-space projections located around the metal cluster region, Figure 4d. Surprisingly, with a water molecule placed close to the defective metal cluster, a considerable number of states are observed in the forbidden region, as shown in Figure 4e. Furthermore, as seen in Figure 4f, the respective real-space projections of these states also show a more pronounced delocalization than before without water (Figure 4d), resulting in a spread to the neighboring pristine metal clusters.
As a proof of concept that water molecules may increase the density of states in the forbidden region, and thus creating a pathway for electrons in HKUST-1, Figure ${4\mathrm{g}}$ shows the pDOs for the situation where two water molecules were placed close to the defective metal cluster. As can be seen, the tendency observed for one is followed for two water molecules, i.e., the pDOs at the Fermi level increases. Hence, as seen in Figure 4h, the projections of such states follow the same behavior, resulting in a further delocalization over the neighboring pristine metal clusters.
Thus, based on the additional states observed in the forbidden region, it is reasonable to suggest that water molecules contribute to the resistive switching since its presence effectively tune the electronic structure of HKUST-1. However, under an external electric field, the respective dipole moments of such molecules may get aligned along the field direction. Hence, this alignment promotes a variation of dipole moments and might render the hydrogen bonds between these molecules less stable, enabling them to move freely. Thus, a possible reorganization of the energy levels around the Fermi level might take place, leading to an enhancement of the electronic properties of the HKUST-1. Furthermore, the water effects on the electronic structure of HKUST-1, associated with the fact of the symmetrical bottom and top gold electrodes, may also explain the ambipolarity observed in Figure 3. Other related effects, including different interfacial situations associated with polarization charges, additional structural defects, and SURMOF homogeneity, may contribute to the resistive switching behavior of HKUST-1 as well.
Figure 5a shows the average of on/off ratios for 10 consecutive switching cycles at different RH levels at VREAD of 0.4 V. As can be seen, the on/off ratios for $90\%$ RH are the most stable, resulting in an average on/off ratio of $7.2\mathrm{x}10^{5}$ , followed by $2.3\mathrm{x}10^{6}$ , and $1.9\mathrm{x}10^{4}$ for $80\%$ , and $70\%$ RH, respectively. Such values are in good agreement with what is observed in Figure 3, and shows how RH influences the on/off ratio. Figure 5b exhibits the on/off ratio for 15 different devices at $90\%$ RH. Most devices have an on/off ratio around $10^{6}$ , which is also compatible with the shown in Figure 3 under the very same RH level but lower VREAD.
Average of on/off ratio for 15 different devices at $90\%$ RH. Devices were chosen randomly between three different chips. (c) Retention characteristics for both resistance states at $90\%$ RH. (d) A comparison of the on/off ratio as a function of $\mathrm{v_{T}}$ from this work to other selected resistive switching devices based on MOFs (solid circles) and SURMOFs (open circles) as an active layer. Data in (a) and (b) were obtained at $\mathrm{V_{READ}}$ of $0.4\mathrm{V}$ .
In order to further explore the resistive switching characteristics, 50 ms squared wave pulses $(+1.3\mathrm{~V~}$ and $-0.3\mathrm{V}$ ) were applied in order to turn the device ON and OFF (LRS and HRS). The current measured at a determined state was monitored as a function of time while VREAD of $0.4\mathrm{V}$ was applied with the device exposed to different RH ( $90\%$ , $80\%$ , and $70\%$ ). It is worth mentioning that no reproducible retention characteristics were observed for RH values $<70\%$ . Conversely, as shown in Figure 5c, at $90\%$ RH, both states can be retained for at least 2000 s. In Figure S9a, obtained at $80\%$ RH, the states are retained for at least 1300 s, followed by 600 s at $70\%$ RH, Figure S9b. Interestingly, after $600\mathrm{~s~}$ in Figure S9b, the retained LRS turns to HRS, showing that RH level plays an essential role in the retention characteristics in SURMOF-based devices. Furthermore, these results suggest that the resistive switching of highly hydrated HKUST-1 SURMOF has a non-volatile nature.
A comparison between the on/off ratio as a function of $\mathrm{v_{T}}$ reported here, and other selected resistive switching devices is shown in Figure 5d. Interestingly, while SURMOF-based devices have $\mathrm{v_{T}}$ around $1\mathrm{V}$ , most of the MOF-based devices have, in general, $\mathrm{v_{T}}$ is considerably higher, except for Zn-TCPP $@$ PVPy (blended)28 and FJU-23- $\mathrm{.H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (crystal)29. This fact is associated with better control of the deposition procedure used for SURMOF films. Hence, the production of reliable films leads to low-power consumption devices. Furthermore, while most of these SURMOF-based devices have presented low on/off ratios, the current Au/SAM-HKUST-1/Au rolled-up vertical heterojunction exhibit a value of $10^{6}$ , close to the highest reported so far, which is based on ZIF-8 MOF.23 However, the ZIF-8-based device has a $\mathrm{v_{T}}$ of $2.5\mathrm{V}$ , while for the rolled-up vertical heterojunction devices is about $0.75\mathrm{V}$ . Thus, the present rNMs-based HKUST-1 SURMOF device mediated by RH levels is promising for applications where lowpower consumption such as memories and environment-responsive electronics is needed.
In summary, we report for the first time on the integration of SURMOF films in rolled-up scalable-functional devices. A freestanding metallic nanomembrane provides a robust and selfadjusted top mechanical contact on the SURMOF layer without damage. The device concept is versatile, fully integrative, compatible with conventional photolithography, and can be used to study numerous porous materials at a nanoscale range under different environmental conditions, e.g., humidity and low-temperatures. A stable and reproducible ambipolar resistive switching mediated by the humidity level was observed in such devices. The on/off ratio under high humidity $(90\%)$ and low threshold voltages $(<1\mathrm{\V})$ is about $10^{6}$ , with a good retention time $10^{3}$ s). The experimental results were interpreted using DFT calculations, suggesting that structural defects associated with the presence of water molecules play a role in the switching characteristics, occasioning the appearance of additional states in the bandgap region. Furthermore, it is worth mentioning that further studies on charge transport mechanisms are in progress considering low-temperatures $(<20\mathrm{k})$ using the same device concept presented here to contact thinner SURMOFs heterojunctions $\mathit{\Theta}_{\mathbf{\tilde{\lambda}}}<10\$ -nm-thick).
Experimental Section. The devices were fabricated on conductive silicon wafers (100) covered by $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ -thick of $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ . The first step was the creation of the finger-like (mesa) structure by patterning and etching $190\mathrm{nm}$ of $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ layer through a reactive ion etching process using $\mathrm{CF}_{4}$ gas, as shown in Figure S1a. Next, the bottom electrode (finger electrode) was formed by depositing Cr/Au $(5/10\mathrm{nm})$ in the mesa region, Figure S1b. All metallic layers were patterned using conventional photolithography with AZ 5214E photoresist and deposited by electron-beam evaporation in a high vacuum ${\sim}10^{-7}$ torr). The distance between the sample and target material in the deposition system is around $40\ \mathrm{cm}$ . Both metallic layers, Cr and Au, of the finger electrode were deposited at $0.5\mathrm{\AA}/\mathrm{s}$ . In the sequence, a 20-nm-thick germanium layer was deposited at 0.2 $\mathrm{{\AA}}/\mathrm{{s}}$ and patterned in front of the mesa region, as shown in Figure 1Sc. The $\mathrm{GeO_{x}}$ sacrificial layer, which is soluble in water, was formed by the oxidation of the germanium layer using a homemade chamber under high RH $(90\%)$ during $16\mathrm{h}$ . The following fabrication process involved the formation of the strained metallic nanomembrane with lateral dimensions of 250 $\upmu\mathrm{m}\upkappa50\upmu\mathrm{m}$ on the top of the $\mathrm{GeO_{x}}$ layer, as shown in Figure 1Sd. The strained metallic layer consists of a tri-metallic Au/Ti/Cr nanomembrane $(5/15/20\mathrm{nm})$ ) deposited at 0.5, 1, and ${\sf5-6}\mathrm{\AA/s}.$ , respectively. The bi-metallic Ti/Cr layer is responsible for creating the strain gradient necessary for the tube electrode formation.38 Then, the contact-pads Cr/Au $20/60\mathrm{nm})$ ) of the strained layer were deposited, both at $1\mathrm{\AA/s}$ , Figure S1e. The next step was the growth and patterning of HKUST-1 SURMOF on the finger electrode followed by the rolled-up process, as shown in Figure S1f-h. Figure S2 shows optical microscopy images of the main fabrication processes. The SURMOF growth was done following the quasi-LPE process,18 a total of 20 deposition cycles were performed. The SURMOF patterning was also performed using conventional photolithography with AZ 5214E photoresist. During the patterning, the region of interest was protected with photoresist. After $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ plasma surface treatment (90 W, 3 mbar) for $10\mathrm{min}$ , the unprotected SURMOF region turned sensitive to organic solvent. Then, immersing the samples in acetone, photoresist and the sensitized SURMOF were removed, keeping the SURMOF patterned on the region of the finger electrode. After the SURMOF patterning on the finger electrode, devices were ready for the rolling-up process. First, a trench region was defined through conventional photolithography to avoid rolling in different directions, as shown in Figure S3a. Figure S3b shows the region defined by the trench. Then, samples were immersed in $0.5\ \%\mathrm{(v/v)}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ aqueous solution for 3 min allowing further $\mathrm{GeO_{x}}$ oxidation without damage the SURMOF layer. Such a process enables the rolling of the strained metallic nanomembrane, which was limited by the trench, as shown in Figure S3c. In the sequence, immersing the samples in acetone, the trench was removed through the photoresist dissolution, and the strained nanomembrane was released from the substrate towards the finger electrode in order to establish the top mechanical contact on the SURMOF film, as shown in Figure S3d-e. Next, the samples were placed in vacuum for at least $24\mathrm{h}$ to dry. Notice that each substrate has $9\mathrm{-}\mathrm{mm}^{2}$ -area with 32 devices, the tube electrode exhibited a typical radius of ${\sim}4~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . The number of rolling windings was calculated as being 2 (see SI).
HKUST-1 SURMOF was growth using the quasi-LPE process, as shown in Figure S4. First, the finger electrode was functionalized using the MHA-SAM. The functionalization occurred by keeping the sample immersed in the ethanol solution with $0.5\mathrm{mM}$ of MHA during $20\mathrm{h}$ . The temperature of the solution was kept at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ followed by $19\mathrm{h}$ in room conditions. With the functionalization completed, the sample was dried using $\Nu_{2}$ . In the sequence, HKUST-1 SURMOF was growth by introducing sequentially the sample in 1mM copper acetate (2 min) and 1mM BTC (4 min) ethanolic solutions, respectively. Pre-washing (1 min) and washing (5 min) ethanolic solutions were used between each solution. After 20 cycles, the sample was dried using $\Nu_{2}$ .
Parallel plate capacitors-based HKUST-1 SURMOF were fabricated on conductive silicon wafers (100) covered by $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ -thick of $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ . First, as the bottom electrode, Cr/Au (20/60 nm) were deposited, both at $0.5\mathrm{\AA}/\mathrm{s}$ , on the entire substrate surface ${9}\mathrm{mm}\mathrm{x}9\mathrm{mm}$ ). Then, samples were functionalized with MHA followed by the 20 or 100 of SURMOF deposition cycles. In the sequence, as the top electrode, Au ( $\mathrm{40~nm})$ was deposited at $0.2{-}0.3\mathrm{\AA/s}$ on the SURMOF surface using a shadow mask, resulting in parallel plate capacitors with circular active areas of about 0.2 $\mathrm{mm}^{2}$ . Then, samples were placed in vacuum for at least $24\mathrm{h}$ to dry. During the electrical characterization, the top contact was performed using a flexible tip in order to avoid any damage to the SURMOF layer.
SEM images were performed using a Field Emission Gun Scanning Electron Microscope (FEG-SEM) Inspect F50 from FEI (Japan). Confocal images were obtained using LSCM VKX200 from Keyence (USA). A Park NX10 AFM was used to obtain topography images, analyses were done with the software Gwyddion. GIXRD patterns of SURMOF were collected using xray synchrotron radiation at the XRD2 beamline in the Brazilian Synchrotron Light Laboratory (LNLS), with incident wavelength $(\uplambda)$ of 1.54979 Å. A Mythen linear detector was used for data acquisition. Diffractograms were obtained between the range from $6^{\circ}$ to $17^{\circ}\left(26\right)$ . The I-V characteristics of the devices were obtained using a conventional probe station equipped with optical microscope and tungsten tips connected in micromanipulators. The micromanipulators have triaxial connectors that were plugged in a Semiconductor Parameter Analyzer 4200-SCS from Keithley (Cleveland, OH, USA). The limit of electrical current that can be measured was $10^{-15}$ A, and a compliance current of $10^{-6}$ A was established. A homemade chamber with a water vapor connection was used to keep the RH levels during the curves. The RH was monitored using a sensor, and their desired levels were obtained by adjusting water vapor and $\Nu_{2}$ gas inside the chamber.
Electronic structure calculations for HKUST-1 were performed considering a triclinic unit cell $\left(\mathbf{a}=18.739\textrm{\AA}\right.$ , $\mathfrak{a}=60^{\circ}$ ). Vienna Ab initio Simulation Package (VASP) was used with the Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) functional approximation for the exchange-correlation energy of DFT equations.51–53 Applying the projector-augmented-wave method,54 the following configurations of valence electrons were considered: C $\mathsf{s}^{2}\mathsf{p}^{2}$ , $\mathrm{{Cud^{10}p^{1}}}$ , $\mathrm{~H~s^{1}~}$ , and O $\mathsf{s}^{2}\mathsf{p}^{4}$ . A plane wave energy cut-off of $400\mathrm{eV}$ was used for all simulations. A Hubbard U parameter of $5\mathrm{eV}$ was employed for Cu d orbitals, according to previous theoretical contributions.49,55 Then, an antiferromagnetic coupling was considered as an initial magnetic configuration for the Cu pairs in the MOF structure. The first step consisted of relaxation of both ionic positions as well as unit cell vectors using Gamma-only sampling, with a convergence criterion of $10^{-3}\mathrm{eV}\mathrm{\AA}^{-1}$ for the respective forces. Next, a single $\mathrm{v_{o}}$ close to Cu atom was introduced per unit cell, and the same structural defect accompanied by one and two water molecules close to the same metallic atom were relaxed keeping the unit cell fixed. For the four relaxed situations, pristine, $\mathrm{V}_{0},\mathrm{V}_{0}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ , and $\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{O}}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , pDOs were obtained with a 4x4x4 Monkhorst-Pack $\mathbf{k}$ -point sampling of the Brillouin Zone. The energy levels real-space projections were obtained integrating all $\mathbf{k}$ -points for a given energy range.
